{"term":"性交","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xìngjiāo","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":22,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The primary Mandarin clinical and legal term for sexual intercourse, equivalent to 'coitus' or 'sexual intercourse' in English.","english_term":"sexual intercourse","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"性 (sex) + 交 (join/exchange); used in medical, legal, and journalistic registers; slightly higher crude score than pure anatomy terms because it names the act directly rather than the organ."}
{"term":"交媾","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiāogòu","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":72,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A formal literary and classical Mandarin term for sexual copulation, used in pre-modern medical texts and literary prose.","english_term":"coitus","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"交 (join) + 媾 (mate/marry); more archaic and formal than 性交; appears in classical novels such as Jin Ping Mei and traditional Chinese medicine texts; understood by educated readers but rarely produced in everyday speech."}
{"term":"交合","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiāohé","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":12,"technical":58,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A literary Mandarin term for sexual union, emphasizing the joining of two bodies; found in classical texts and traditional medicine.","english_term":"union","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"交 (join/intersect) + 合 (combine/unite); softer and more poetic than 交媾; used in Taoist and traditional medical discourse about the harmony of yin and yang energies during intercourse."}
{"term":"性行为","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xìng xíngwéi","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"sexual_activity_general","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":82,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Mandarin clinical phrase for 'sexual behavior' or 'sexual act,' broader than intercourse alone; the default neutral term in public health, legal, and research discourse.","english_term":"sexual behavior","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"性 (sex) + 行为 (behavior/conduct); technically covers all sexual acts, not only penetrative intercourse; preferred register for policy documents, sex education curricula, and academic research."}
{"term":"性爱","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xìng'ài","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_romantic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":18,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Neutral-to-positive Mandarin term for lovemaking or sexual love, combining the physical and emotional dimensions; the everyday equivalent of 'making love.'","english_term":"lovemaking","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"性 (sex/nature) + 爱 (love); warmer connotation than 性交; widely used in popular novels, film, and everyday speech; lacks clinical coldness and crude vulgarity."}
{"term":"房事","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fángshì","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":10,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A domestic euphemism for sexual intercourse, especially within marriage; literally 'bedroom affairs,' common in traditional Chinese medicine and popular health writing.","english_term":"bedroom activities","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"房 (room/chamber) + 事 (affairs/matters); associated with TCM discussions of marital health and sexual restraint; carries a slightly conservative or older-generation register; still widely recognized but less produced by younger speakers."}
{"term":"性关系","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xìng guānxi","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"sexual_relationship","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":12,"technical":62,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Clinical and neutral Mandarin phrase for a sexual relationship or the act of having sexual relations with someone.","english_term":"sexual relationship","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"性 (sex) + 关系 (relationship); the standard journalistic formulation '发生性关系' (to enter a sexual relationship / to have sex) is ubiquitous in news reporting on sexual assault, affairs, and legal cases."}
{"term":"肉体关系","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"ròutǐ guānxi","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"sexual_relationship","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":22,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Euphemistic Mandarin phrase for a sexual/carnal relationship; frames intercourse in terms of physical flesh rather than the clinical or romantic registers.","english_term":"physical relationship","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"肉体 (flesh/body) + 关系 (relationship); 肉体 carries a more carnal, occasionally pejorative nuance absent from 性关系; often implies an illicit, transactional, or purely physical arrangement rather than a loving one."}
{"term":"操","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cào","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":88,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most common Mandarin crude verb meaning 'to fuck'; used both as a transitive verb for sexual intercourse and as a standalone expletive expressing anger or surprise.","english_term":"fuck","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Homophonous with 操 (to operate/drill/exercise), enabling deliberate spelling substitution online; functions as both a sex verb and a general profanity; the single most frequently used Mandarin expletive across all age groups."}
{"term":"肏","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cào","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":97,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The graphically explicit character for 'to fuck,' composed of 入 (enter) over 肉 (flesh); the etymologically correct vulgar character for the sex act.","english_term":"fuck","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Distinct from the homophone 操 (operate); 肏 is consistently blocked by Chinese content filters, driving much lower digital-text frequency despite high recognition among educated users; its visual composition makes its meaning self-evident to literate readers."}
{"term":"干","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gàn","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Polysemous Mandarin verb meaning 'to do/work' in standard usage and 'to fuck' in crude sexual contexts; one of several standard-character sex euphemisms.","english_term":"do (someone)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Standard meaning is entirely non-sexual; sexual sense activated by context; lower crude score than 操 because the character itself is neutral and plausible deniability is high; common in Northern spoken Mandarin."}
{"term":"草","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cǎo","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Originally 'grass/straw'; adopted as a phonetically similar substitute for 操 (cào) to evade internet censorship filters, and now used as a standalone crude expletive.","english_term":"fuck","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Tonal difference from 操 (3rd tone vs 4th tone) provides minimal cover; rose to dominance in the 2010s alongside the expansion of Chinese social media censorship; also functions as a pure exclamation of shock or displeasure independent of sexual meaning."}
{"term":"搞","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"gǎo","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":28,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A versatile Mandarin verb meaning 'to do/handle/deal with' in standard use; used as a mild euphemism for having sex in spoken and written sexual contexts.","english_term":"do (someone)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most common neutral-to-crude euphemisms for sex; strong plausible deniability from its non-sexual standard meaning; '搞在一起' ('got together') can mean began a romantic or sexual relationship."}
{"term":"上","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"shàng","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":62,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Standard Mandarin preposition and verb meaning 'above/on/to go up'; used crudely to mean 'to have sex with,' implying the active mounting role.","english_term":"get on (someone)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Encodes positional dominance — the active partner 'goes on top of' the passive; '上了她' literally 'went up on her'; standard meaning is entirely non-sexual, giving high deniability; framing implies male or dominant-party agency."}
{"term":"弄","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nòng","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_generic","concept":"intercourse_generic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":22,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Mandarin verb meaning 'to play with/handle/manipulate'; used as a mild euphemism for sexual activity, encompassing foreplay and intercourse depending on context.","english_term":"mess around with (someone)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Standard meaning is entirely non-sexual; softer register than 操 or 干; contextually ambiguous between foreplay and full intercourse; common in intimate spoken language and literary fiction."}
{"term":"日","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rì","category":"acts","subcategory":"intercourse_aggressive","concept":"intercourse_aggressive","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":"CN","definition":"Crude Mandarin slang for 'to fuck,' using the standard character for 'sun/day' (rì) as a homophone substitution; predominantly Northern Chinese usage.","english_term":"fuck","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Phonetically identical to 日 meaning 'sun/day'; sexual meaning is predominantly a Northern Mainland Chinese phenomenon (especially Henan, Shaanxi, Shandong dialects); understood but rarely produced in Southern China and Taiwan; functions as both a transitive sex verb and a general expletive in Northern speech."}
